# .gitignore
# KIT-CLASS: MIXED — the kit's own entries are real and complete; the build-artifact
# section is a marked fill-in. See process/EXTRACTION.md.

# ── Secrets — never commit ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only .env.example is tracked. See PROJECT.md § Credential doctrine.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── Process kit: the auxiliary trunk checkout and its locks ─────────────────
# The board scripts do all kanban git operations in a standing detached worktree
# pinned to the trunk, so a feature-branch checkout never has to leave its branch
# to move a card. It is machinery, never source.
.kanban-wt/
.kanban-wt.lock/
.kanban-wt.lock.reap/

# ── Process kit: the per-session declared role (the hat gate) ───────────────
# Ephemeral: one line naming the hat this session is wearing. Never committed.
.claude/session-role

# ── Process kit: isolation worktrees ───────────────────────────────────────
.worktrees/
.claude/worktrees/

# ── Process kit: bytecode from the hygiene instruments ─────────────────────
# scripts/hygiene/ ships advisory Python instruments (standard library only,
# never a gate). Their supported entry points set sys.dont_write_bytecode before
# importing each other, so the normal path writes nothing — but a bare
# `python -c "import ..."` or a REPL session writes scripts/hygiene/__pycache__/
# anyway, and CPython writes a module's own .pyc before its body can stop it.
# Ignored because that residue is not yours and has one nasty property: a guard
# that walks scripts/ and fails on a file with no KIT-CLASS marker REDDENS on the
# .pyc while git status stays clean. See scripts/hygiene/citation_index.py's
# header, which also prescribes `rm -rf scripts/hygiene/__pycache__`.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ── OS / editor cruft ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# ── Logs and scratch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
/tmp/
/scratch/

# ── <your build artifacts> ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FILL ME. One line per generated tree or file your toolchain produces, with a
# comment saying WHAT produces it — an unexplained ignore line is indistinguishable
# from a mistake, and the next reader will not know whether deleting it is safe.
#
# Two rules that are not obvious:
#   • Never add a blanket ignore for a data format your project TRACKS on purpose
#     (a `*.json` catch-all is the classic way to lose a committed fixture or a
#     package manifest).
#   • If your build stages a copy of the source tree somewhere before packaging it,
#     ignore that staging path by name — repo-walking guards honour this file, and
#     a mid-build copy will otherwise be scanned as if it were source.
#
# <e.g. dist/            # produced by <your build command>>
# <e.g. build/           # produced by <your build command>>
# <e.g. <dep-dir>/       # installed by ./setup.sh from the committed lockfile>
# <e.g. <cache-dir>/     # test-runner cache>
